    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Most roof estimates are written in Xactimate, line by line. Each line is a quantity times a unit price, so small errors add up.</li>
<li>Check the measurements first: roof area in squares, waste factor, and linear feet of eaves, rakes, ridges, hips and valleys.</li>
<li>Commonly missed items include starter, drip edge, ridge cap, flashing, vents, steep and high-roof charges, and code-required items.</li>
<li>Overhead and profit and matching depend on the scope of the repair and your policy.</li>
<li>Request a supplement in writing, item by item, with photos and measurements attached.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>If the insurance check for your roof will not cover a contractor’s bid, the problem is often hiding in the estimate itself. Learning <strong>how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</strong> line by line can help you find what was missed, mismeasured or priced too low, and ask for it in a way an adjuster can act on. This guide walks through a typical Xactimate roof estimate section by section, with a checklist table you can use on your own claim.</p>

<p>It is written for homeowners and property owners in San Marcos, Hays County and nearby Central Texas communities, where hail and wind claims are common. It is general information, not legal advice.</p>

<h2>Start with the estimate summary and your policy</h2>

<p>Before you look at individual lines, read the summary page. It usually shows the replacement cost (RCV), depreciation, actual cash value (ACV), deductible and net payment. If the gap between your contractor’s bid and the insurer’s number is mostly depreciation and deductible, the issue may be how the policy pays, not the estimate itself. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains that difference.</p>

<p>Then check your declarations page for how the roof is covered (replacement cost, ACV or a roof payment schedule), your wind/hail deductible, and whether you have ordinance or law coverage. Those three facts frame everything else.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/xactimate-estimate-review-desk.webp" alt="Adjuster at two monitors reviewing an estimate and roof photos" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Review the estimate next to the roof photos and measurements, not on its own.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Line-by-line review of a roof insurance estimate</h2>

<h3>1. Roof measurements and squares</h3>
<p>A roofing square is 100 square feet. The estimate should show the total roof area, usually from a sketch or an aerial measurement report, and the number of squares for tear-off and for new shingles. Compare it with an independent measurement. Look for slopes left out of the sketch, porch or garage roofs that were skipped, and pitch values that are lower than the real roof. Pitch affects area, so an understated pitch understates the squares.</p>

<h3>2. Waste factor</h3>
<p>Removal is measured at the actual roof area, but new shingles have to include waste for cutting at hips, valleys, rakes and penetrations. A simple gable roof needs less waste than a cut-up roof with many hips and valleys. If the estimate applies a low waste percentage to a complex roof, or no waste at all, that can be a meaningful shortfall. Ask what waste factor was used and how it was chosen.</p>

<h3>3. Starter course</h3>
<p>Many shingle manufacturers call for a starter strip along the eaves, and some also call for it along the rakes. Check that starter appears as its own line with linear feet that match the eaves (and rakes, if the manufacturer requires it). Estimates sometimes assume starter is covered by waste, which may not be consistent with how the roof is actually installed.</p>

<h3>4. Ridge and hip cap</h3>
<p>The estimate should include ridge or hip cap shingles for the full linear feet of ridges and hips. Laminated (architectural) shingle roofs typically use manufactured hip and ridge cap, which is priced differently from cut three-tab pieces. Compare the linear feet with your measurement report.</p>

<h3>5. Drip edge</h3>
<p>The IRC requires drip edge at the eaves and rake edges of asphalt shingle roofs. Check that drip edge is included for the full eave and rake length. If the old roof had none, replacing the roof may still require it, which ties into code items below.</p>

<h3>6. Underlayment</h3>
<p>The adopted IRC requires underlayment under roof coverings. Confirm the estimate includes underlayment for the full area and that the type matches what the installation requires.</p>

<h3>7. Flashing</h3>
<p>Look for step flashing along walls, counterflashing, chimney flashing, valley metal if used, and headwall or apron flashing. Flashing is often damaged or bent during tear-off, and reusing old flashing may not meet code or manufacturer instructions. If the estimate does not mention flashing, ask why.</p>

<h3>8. Vents and penetrations</h3>
<p>Count every roof penetration: pipe jacks (plumbing vent boots), box or turtle vents, ridge vent, turbine vents, power vents, exhaust caps and furnace or water heater flues. Hail commonly dents soft metal vents, and each one should have a line. Detach-and-reset items such as satellite dishes or solar equipment should also appear if they must be moved.</p>

<h3>9. Steep and high-roof charges</h3>
<p>Working on a steep roof or a tall roof takes longer and requires more safety equipment. Xactimate has separate additional charges for steep roofs (by pitch range, typically starting at 7/12) and for high roofs (generally two stories or more). If your roof is steep or two stories and these charges are missing, that can be a significant gap. Check that they are applied to the right slopes and quantities, for both removal and installation.</p>

<h3>10. Tear-off, layers, decking and disposal</h3>
<p>If there are two existing layers, the estimate should price removal of both. Check for debris removal or dumpster charges, and for decking replacement where the decking is damaged or deteriorated. Decking problems are often discovered only during tear-off, which is a common reason for a supplement.</p>

[[cta]]

<h3>11. Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</h3>
<p>Overhead and profit covers a general contractor’s costs and margin for coordinating the job. It is often written as 10% overhead plus 10% profit. Insurers generally include it when the repair reasonably requires a general contractor, for example when several trades are involved (roofing, gutters, siding, painting, interior repairs). It is not owed automatically on every claim, but if your repair involves multiple trades and O&amp;P is missing, ask the adjuster to explain the decision in writing.</p>

<h3>12. Code-required items</h3>
<p>The City of San Marcos enforces the 2021 International Codes, in effect since April 1, 2023. A local amendment means a roof covering replacement usually does not need a permit unless the new roof is heavier than the old one, but the work is still expected to meet code. Items the adopted IRC and the manufacturer require should be in the estimate. Whether upgrades beyond a like-kind repair are paid usually depends on ordinance or law coverage. Our article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">building codes and insurance repairs in San Marcos</a> covers this in detail, and our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes page</a> lists permit offices for nearby cities.</p>

<h3>13. Matching</h3>
<p>If the insurer approves only some slopes, ask whether the replacement shingles will reasonably match the rest of the roof in color, style and size. Discontinued shingles are a common issue. Whether the insurer must pay to replace undamaged areas to achieve a match depends on your policy language and the facts. Document the shingle brand and line, any evidence it is discontinued, and photos showing the visible difference.</p>

<h3>14. Unit prices, labor minimums and the price list</h3>
<p>Estimates use a price list for a specific region and month. Confirm the price list matches your area and the time of the loss or repair. Watch for items priced as a repair when a replacement is needed, and for labor minimums omitted on small trades.</p>

<h2>Roof estimate review checklist</h2>

