<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you collect the photos and records listed above.</p>

<h2>Check the damage scope, not just the line items</h2>

<p>Sometimes the estimate is accurate for what it covers, but it covers too little: a few slopes or a "spot repair" when hail damage is spread across the roof. Compare the approved scope with photos from every slope. Hail damage in Hays County can be significant. NOAA records 58 hail reports of golf-ball size or larger in the county since 1967, and on <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181409" target="_blank" rel="noopener">May 9, 2024</a> it logged 2.5-inch hail near the outlet mall in San Marcos. Dated storm records from our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> can help connect the damage to a specific event. For more on hail, see our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>How to request a roof supplement</h2>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/estimate-software-roof-diagram.webp" alt="Adjuster using estimating software with a roof diagram on a laptop" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A supplement request works best when each missing item has a quantity, a reason and a photo.</figcaption></figure>

<ol>
<li><strong>List each item separately.</strong> Name the missing or underpaid line, the quantity and the unit (squares, linear feet, each).</li>
<li><strong>Explain why it is needed.</strong> Cite the manufacturer instruction, code requirement or condition found on the roof.</li>
<li><strong>Attach evidence.</strong> Photos, measurement reports, invoices and any building official correspondence.</li>
<li><strong>Send it in writing</strong> to the adjuster assigned to your claim, and keep a copy.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for a revised estimate</strong> or a written explanation for any item they decline.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up on time.</strong> Texas prompt-payment rules set timeframes for insurers to respond once they have the information they requested. Keep track of dates.</li>
</ol>

<p>If additional damage or code items appear during tear-off, document them with photos before the new roof covers them up, and submit them promptly. Our article on <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements/">hail damage claim supplements</a> and our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> page go deeper. If the disagreement is over the amount of loss and cannot be resolved, appraisal may be available under your policy; see <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>.</p>

<h2>Getting a second set of eyes from a San Marcos public adjuster</h2>

<p>Reviewing an estimate takes time and familiarity with how insurance estimates are built. A <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can compare the insurer’s estimate with the roof, prepare a detailed estimate in the same format, and present the differences with the evidence behind them. Public adjusters are licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance and represent the policyholder only. We cannot promise a higher payment, and legal questions about coverage belong with a Texas attorney.</p>

<p>If your roof estimate looks short, our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> pages explain how we work. A San Marcos public adjuster can review your estimate at no cost through our <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
